PYTHON EXAMPLE - UDP SEND (ONLY WITH AUTHORIZATION)

Network operations can affect actual devices. Inspect /man/network,
check the target service and obtain authorization before sending.
Create /tmp/udp.py:

  s = socket(2)
  if s < 0:
      print("socket failed")
  else:
      n = sendto(s, "hello", "127.0.0.1", 9000)
      print(n)
      close(s)

Run: python /tmp/udp.py
Expected: byte count or error status, NOT proof of UDP reception.

In this interpreter numeric socket(2) means datagram; names such as
SOCK_DGRAM are not automatically defined Python expression values.
127.0.0.1 is loopback. Port 9000 is an EXAMPLE, not a guaranteed
listener. Do not send to UDP 2000 without verifying device-control
protocol and user intent. For interactive/blocking servers, use a
physical terminal instead of MCP's 3-second execute_command.
